1. Purpose and Scope
This guide defines the technical configuration for managing Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) Administrator passwords with CyberArk CPM by invoking Microsoft NTDSUTIL. The solution is intended to support controlled Change and Reconcile workflows for Domain Controller DSRM passwords while avoiding unsafe online Verify behavior.
The guide covers CPM prerequisites, NTDSUTIL installation, file placement, CyberArk platform settings, account onboarding, linked-account considerations, validation, testing, troubleshooting, and operational guardrails.
In Scope	Out of Scope
One vaulted CyberArk account per Domain Controller DSRM Administrator password	Changing normal domain user passwords
CyberArk CPM Change and Reconcile using NTDSUTIL	Booting Domain Controllers into DSRM mode for routine verification
RSAT / AD DS Tools installation guidance on CPM	Replacing Microsoft domain recovery procedures
Lab validation workflow and production hardening checklist	Guaranteed drop-in import for every CyberArk CPM/TPC version without local syntax validation
2. Solution Overview
The solution uses a CyberArk custom CPM platform that calls a PowerShell wrapper. The wrapper starts ntdsutil.exe and submits the supported DSRM reset sequence through redirected standard input. The target account represents the DSRM Administrator password for one Domain Controller.
Component	Role
CyberArk Vault / PVWA	Stores the DSRM account object and platform configuration.
CyberArk CPM	Executes the custom platform action and records the result.
Invoke-DSRMPasswordReset.ps1	PowerShell 5.1 wrapper that drives ntdsutil.exe and redacts the generated password from logs.
ntdsutil.exe	Microsoft utility used to reset the DSRM Administrator password.
Domain Controller	Target system whose local DSRM password is reset.
CyberArk Account --> CPM Platform --> PowerShell Wrapper --> ntdsutil.exe --> Target Domain Controller
       Address = DC FQDN            NewPassword             reset password on server <Address>
3. DSRM and NTDSUTIL Background
DSRM is the Directory Services Restore Mode local Administrator password on each Domain Controller. It is separate from normal domain accounts and should be treated as a per-DC recovery credential. Microsoft documents that newer Windows Server versions integrate this reset capability into NTDSUTIL, and that ntdsutil.exe can reset the password on the server being worked on or another Domain Controller in the domain.
The Microsoft-supported command pattern is interactive and includes selecting the DSRM password context, choosing the server, entering the new password, and confirming it. The wrapper automates this interactive sequence for CyberArk CPM.
ntdsutil
set dsrm password
reset password on server <DomainControllerFQDN>
<new password>
<confirm new password>
quit
quit
Verify Limitation: A true DSRM password verification normally requires booting the Domain Controller into Directory Services Restore Mode and logging in as .\Administrator. This is not appropriate for routine CPM verification. Configure Verify as disabled or prerequisite-only.
4. Architecture and Account Model
4.1 Vaulted DSRM Account
CyberArk Field	Recommended Value	Notes
Platform	WIN_DSRM_NTDSUTIL	Custom CPM platform for DSRM via NTDSUTIL.
Address	dc01.contoso.com	Target Domain Controller hostname or FQDN.
Username	Administrator	DSRM local Administrator username.
Password	Current DSRM password	Initial value must be correct before CPM can rotate from the stored value model.
Safe	Dedicated DSRM safe	Use least privilege and strict audit controls.
4.2 One Account Per Domain Controller
DSRM is local to each Domain Controller. Do not vault one shared DSRM account for an entire domain. Create one account object per DC so audit, rotation status, and reconciliation outcomes remain system-specific.
Example CyberArk objects:
  DC01 DSRM  -> Address dc01.contoso.com, Username Administrator
  DC02 DSRM  -> Address dc02.contoso.com, Username Administrator
  DC03 DSRM  -> Address dc03.contoso.com, Username Administrator
4.3 Linked Accounts / Execution Identity
The starter wrapper does not consume a logon password directly. NTDSUTIL runs under the Windows security context of the CPM process or the configured platform execution identity. For production, validate the rights model in the target environment. The CPM service account or run-as account must have the required administrative rights to reset DSRM remotely on the target Domain Controller.
Design Decision: Use an explicitly approved administrative execution identity. Do not assume that the vaulted DSRM password itself can perform the reset; DSRM is a recovery-mode credential, not a normal domain admin credential.
5. Prerequisites
Category	Requirement	Validation
CyberArk	CPM installed and able to run custom platform actions.	Confirm CPM service health and existing custom plugin execution.
Operating System	Windows Server CPM with PowerShell 5.1.	$PSVersionTable.PSVersion
Microsoft Tools	ntdsutil.exe available on CPM.	where ntdsutil or Get-Command ntdsutil.exe
Network	CPM can resolve and reach each target Domain Controller.	DNS lookup and TCP/RPC reachability test.
Permissions	Execution identity can reset DSRM password on remote DC.	Lab reset against non-production DC.
Change Control	Approved maintenance process for DSRM password changes.	Change record and rollback plan.
6. CPM Server NTDSUTIL Installation
Install the Active Directory Domain Services / AD LDS RSAT tools on the CPM server so ntdsutil.exe is available. Microsoft lists NTDSUtil.exe as part of the AD DS and AD LDS RSAT tools, and Microsoft RSAT guidance for Windows Server uses Install-WindowsFeature with RSAT-AD-Tools.
# Windows Server CPM - run PowerShell as Administrator
Install-WindowsFeature -Name RSAT-AD-Tools -IncludeAllSubFeature

# Validate
where ntdsutil
Get-Command ntdsutil.exe
# Windows 10/11 style host, if applicable
Add-WindowsCapability -Online -Name 'Rsat.ActiveDirectory.DS-LDS.Tools~~~~0.0.1.0' 
6.1 Package Installer Script
The package includes Install-DSRMNtdsutilPrereqs.ps1. On Windows Server, run it without the WindowsClient switch. On a Windows client-style host, include -WindowsClient.
# Windows Server CPM
.\Install-DSRMNtdsutilPrereqs.ps1

# Windows client-style host
.\Install-DSRMNtdsutilPrereqs.ps1 -WindowsClient
7. Plugin Package Contents
File	Purpose
Invoke-DSRMPasswordReset.ps1	PowerShell wrapper used by CPM to run NTDSUTIL and submit the DSRM reset sequence. Redacts the generated password from logs.
Test-DSRMNtdsutilPrereqs.ps1	Validates ntdsutil.exe presence, current execution identity, DNS resolution, and optional TCP/RPC reachability.
Install-DSRMNtdsutilPrereqs.ps1	Installs RSAT AD tools so ntdsutil.exe is available.
DSRM_NTDSUTIL_Process.ini	Starter TPC process file mapping Change, Reconcile, and Verify actions to PowerShell wrapper calls.
DSRM_NTDSUTIL_Prompts.ini	Starter prompt file if the environment chooses direct prompt matching against ntdsutil.exe.
Policy-WIN_DSRM_NTDSUTIL.ini	Starter CPM platform policy values.
Policy-WIN_DSRM_NTDSUTIL.xml	Starter platform metadata and property definitions.
README-DSRM-NTDSUTIL-CyberArk.txt	Package usage summary and operational notes.
8. CyberArk Platform Configuration
8.1 Platform Import Strategy
The included policy files are starter metadata. Use an exported, working Windows custom CPM platform from the target CyberArk environment as the authoritative base and merge the values below. CyberArk TPC/process-file keywords can differ between CPM versions and customer implementations, so validate syntax against existing working custom platforms.
Setting	Recommended Value	Rationale
PolicyID	WIN_DSRM_NTDSUTIL	Clear unique platform identifier.
MaxConcurrentConnections	1	DSRM reset operations should be serialized and controlled.
AllowManualChange	Yes	Allows controlled one-off password reset.
PerformPeriodicChange	No initially	Enable only after lab and pilot validation.
VFAllowManualVerification	No or prereq-only	True DSRM verification is not safe during normal operations.
RCAllowManualReconciliation	Yes	Allows recovery if stored password is out of sync.
RCAutomaticReconcileWhenUnsynched	No initially	Avoid unexpected DSRM resets until production process is approved.
PasswordLength	20 or environment standard	Use strong recovery password standard.
PasswordForbiddenChars	Avoid characters that break NTDSUTIL/TPC parsing	Tune after testing with local prompt behavior.
8.2 Process File Starter Values
[Change]
Command=powershell.exe
Arguments=-NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "Invoke-DSRMPasswordReset.ps1" -Action Change -TargetServer "{Address}" -NewPassword "{NewPassword}"
Timeout=120

[Reconcile]
Command=powershell.exe
Arguments=-NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "Invoke-DSRMPasswordReset.ps1" -Action Reconcile -TargetServer "{Address}" -NewPassword "{NewPassword}"
Timeout=120

[Verify]
Command=powershell.exe
Arguments=-NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "Invoke-DSRMPasswordReset.ps1" -Action Verify -TargetServer "{Address}"
Timeout=60
Local Syntax Validation: Replace {Address} and {NewPassword} placeholders with the exact placeholder syntax used by the organization’s existing CyberArk CPM/TPC samples if they differ.
9. Deployment Procedure
Step	Action	Expected Result
1	Extract the package to a staging folder on the CPM server.	All package files are visible and access-controlled.
2	Install RSAT AD tools and confirm ntdsutil.exe is available.	where ntdsutil returns a path.
3	Create a controlled plugin folder, for example C:\Program Files (x86)\CyberArk\Password Manager\bin\DSRM_NTDSUTIL\.	Folder exists with restricted permissions.
4	Copy Invoke-DSRMPasswordReset.ps1 and support scripts into the plugin folder.	CPM can read and execute wrapper.
5	Merge starter policy/process values into a working CyberArk custom platform export.	Platform imports or updates successfully.
6	Create a test safe and onboard one non-production DC DSRM account.	Account exists with Address = DC FQDN and Username = Administrator.
7	Run prereq validation from CPM server.	ntdsutil found; DNS/RPC reachability validated.
8	Run manual lab Change; then inspect wrapper logs and CPM result.	Password reset succeeds and no password is exposed in logs.
9	Pilot with limited production scope after CAB approval.	Controlled rollout begins with audit trail.
10. Validation and Testing
10.1 CPM-Side Prerequisite Test
.\Test-DSRMNtdsutilPrereqs.ps1 -TargetServer dc01.contoso.com
10.2 Manual Lab Change Test
.\Invoke-DSRMPasswordReset.ps1 `
  -Action Change `
  -TargetServer dc01.contoso.com `
  -NewPassword 'NewPasswordHere!12345' 
10.3 Local DC Test Pattern
.\Invoke-DSRMPasswordReset.ps1 `
  -Action Change `
  -UseLocalNullServer `
  -NewPassword 'NewPasswordHere!12345' 
10.4 CyberArk CPM Test
Test	Action	Pass Criteria
Verify/prereq	Run platform Verify if enabled as prerequisite-only.	Returns success only for ntdsutil availability, not actual DSRM authentication.
Change	Run manual Change from PVWA on lab DC account.	CPM completes successfully; NTDSUTIL reports successful reset.
Reconcile	Run manual Reconcile with known generated password on lab DC account.	CPM completes successfully and vault updates new password.
Logging	Review wrapper log folder.	No generated password appears in clear text.
Failure Handling	Test against invalid DC name in lab.	Failure is captured clearly and CPM marks action failed.
11. Operations Runbook
11.1 Onboarding a New Domain Controller DSRM Account
1.	Confirm the Domain Controller hostname/FQDN and environment owner.
2.	Confirm the current DSRM password with the authorized Windows/AD team.
3.	Create or select the approved DSRM safe.
4.	Onboard the account with Platform = WIN_DSRM_NTDSUTIL, Address = DC FQDN, Username = Administrator.
5.	Disable automatic periodic change until the account passes lab/pilot validation.
6.	Run prerequisite-only Verify if enabled, then run a controlled Change after approval.
11.2 Performing a Manual Change
7.	Create or reference an approved change ticket.
8.	Confirm no domain recovery or DC maintenance is in progress.
9.	Run Change from PVWA against one Domain Controller account.
10.	Review CPM and wrapper logs for successful NTDSUTIL completion.
11.	Record the outcome in the change ticket and proceed to the next DC only after validation.
11.3 Reconcile Guidance
Use Reconcile when the vaulted DSRM password is suspected to be out of sync. Because Reconcile can reset the DSRM password without relying on the current vaulted password, it should be restricted to approved PAM engineers or delegated operations staff. Keep automatic reconcile disabled until a production support model is approved.
12. Security, Audit, and Change Control
Control Area	Recommendation
Safe Membership	Restrict retrieve/use/update/ownership permissions to the PAM engineering and approved break-glass teams.
Execution Rights	Use a dedicated, approved administrative execution identity with documented rights. Avoid broad unmanaged Domain Admin usage where possible.
Password Policy	Use strong passwords aligned with domain recovery standards. Validate special characters with NTDSUTIL/TPC before enabling broad rotation.
Logging	Retain CPM logs and wrapper sanitized logs. Confirm generated passwords are redacted.
Change Control	Require CAB/change approval before production bulk rotation.
Concurrency	Keep MaxConcurrentConnections = 1 for the platform to avoid simultaneous resets across multiple DCs.
Verification	Do not attempt true DSRM authentication verification during normal operations because it requires DSRM boot mode.
13. Troubleshooting Matrix
Symptom	Likely Cause	Recommended Action
ntdsutil.exe not found	RSAT AD DS / AD LDS tools missing or PATH not refreshed.	Install RSAT-AD-Tools; reopen PowerShell; validate Get-Command ntdsutil.exe.
Access is denied	CPM execution identity lacks required rights.	Validate account rights with Windows/AD team; test manually from CPM under the same identity.
RPC server unavailable	Network/firewall/DNS issue to DC.	Validate DNS, RPC port 135, dynamic RPC requirements, and server availability.
Password complexity failure	Generated password violates local/domain policy or prompt parsing breaks special chars.	Tune CyberArk password policy and forbidden characters; retest in lab.
CPM timeout	NTDSUTIL prompt did not complete or network latency is high.	Increase TimeoutSeconds and review sanitized raw output log.
Verify succeeds but password still unknown	Verify is prerequisite-only by design.	Use controlled Change/Reconcile; true verification requires DSRM boot mode.
Platform import issues	Starter XML/INI does not match local CyberArk platform export expectations.	Merge settings into a known-good exported custom platform rather than importing blindly.
14. Rollback Plan
Rollback for a DSRM reset means resetting the DSRM password again to an approved known value. Because the old value may no longer be valid after change, rollback should be treated as a controlled Reconcile operation with authorized change approval.
12.	Stop any scheduled CPM rotation for the affected DSRM platform.
13.	Confirm the target Domain Controller and change window.
14.	Use manual Reconcile to set a known approved password.
15.	Validate NTDSUTIL output and update the change record.
16.	Resume CPM scheduling only after root cause is understood.
